Spring Boot E-Commerce Application — Project Plan
Status: Planned documentation. This file describes the intended Spring Boot e-commerce application; it does not describe the existing Weather Forecast App files in this repository as e-commerce functionality.
Goal
Build a learning-focused e-commerce REST API with Java and Spring Boot. The project will grow incrementally from a maintainable monolith into a more advanced application as new concepts are implemented.
Planned technology stack
Language: Java
Backend: Spring Boot, Spring Web
Persistence: Spring Data JPA / Hibernate
Database: MySQL
Security: Spring Security with database-backed users and roles; JWT can be added in a later phase
API testing: Postman
Build tool: Maven
Version control: Git and GitHub
Containerization (later phase): Docker and Docker Compose
These are intended choices, not a claim that the current repository already contains this stack.
Planned features
Product catalog
Create, list, view, update, and delete products
Validate required fields and prices
Support pagination and sorting in a later iteration
Categories
Organize products by category
Define the product/category relationship
Users and security
Register users and store passwords securely using a password encoder
Authenticate users through Spring Security
Add roles and restrict management endpoints
Add JWT authentication only after the basic security flow is understood
Shopping cart
Add and remove products
Change item quantities
Calculate totals on the server
Orders
Create orders from a cart
Preserve product prices at the time of purchase
Track order status
Testing and operations
Unit and integration tests
Consistent error responses and logging
API documentation
Docker-based local development and deployment guidance
Suggested domain model
User — account details, encoded password, and role
Product — name, description, price, stock quantity, and category
Category — category name and description
Cart — owner and cart items
CartItem — product and quantity
Order — owner, status, totals, and creation timestamp
OrderItem — product reference, quantity, and unit-price snapshot
Use database constraints and server-side validation. Store money as a decimal type (for example, Java BigDecimal), not floating point. The exact schema should be finalized as each feature is implemented.
Proposed implementation roadmap
Create the Spring Boot project and configure MySQL.
Implement the Product entity, repository, service, and REST controller.
Add request validation, exception handling, pagination, and tests.
Add categories and define relationships.
Implement database-backed users and Spring Security.
Add role-based authorization for product-management endpoints.
Build cart and order workflows with transaction boundaries.
Add API documentation and integration tests.
Containerize the application with Docker and document deployment.
Consider service separation only after the core application is stable.
Local setup (once the backend is implemented)
Install a compatible JDK, Maven (or use the Maven wrapper), and MySQL.
Configure the database URL, username, and password using environment variables or a local, untracked configuration file.
Never commit real passwords, API keys, or production secrets.
Start the application with ./mvnw spring-boot:run (Linux/macOS) or mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run (Windows), once a Maven project has been added.
Verify the implemented endpoints using Postman and the project's tests.
